# DashLLM configuration — ALL OPTIONAL.
#
#   ./launch.sh          # works with no .env at all
#   cp .env.example .env # only if you want to change something below
#
# Read by ./launch.sh and by the backend directly (app/config.py points at this
# repo's .env). If you install the systemd units they read the same file. Keep
# the syntax to the subset everything understands: KEY=value, no `export`, no
# $VAR expansion, no inline comments after a value. Quote values with spaces.
#
# There is no API key and no admin password: relay is an open OpenAI-compatible
# endpoint. What bounds it is the model policy — only OpenCode models with
# "free" in the id are servable (an endpoint you add with your own paid key,
# such as a hosted provider, is spent by whoever reaches the relay). See
# docs/deployment.md before exposing this beyond a network you trust.

# ---- relay ---------------------------------------------------------------
RELAY_PORT=4000

# Bind address. 127.0.0.1, this host only, is the default: the backend's own
# default, launch.sh's, and what AIHub sets in service.toml ([run.env] wins over
# this file under AIHub). Don't change it to 0.0.0.0: there is no API key, and
# the OpenCode endpoint runs shell commands for whoever calls it. See
# docs/deployment.md before exposing the relay beyond this machine.
#RELAY_HOST=127.0.0.1

# ---- opencode ------------------------------------------------------------
# Set to 0 to launch relay on its own.
OPENCODE_ENABLED=1
OPENCODE_PORT=4096

# HTTP Basic credentials the agent server demands and relay presents. Leave
# both unset and launch.sh generates a password on first run, storing it in
# web/backend/data/opencode-auth.env (gitignored). Set them to pin your own.
#OPENCODE_SERVER_USERNAME=opencode
#OPENCODE_SERVER_PASSWORD=

# The project the agent works in. `opencode serve` is bound to one directory —
# its bash/edit/write tools act here. Defaults to this repo, which is rarely
# what you want; point it at the project you actually want the agent driving.
#OPENCODE_PROJECT_DIR=

# How the endpoint appears in relay. OPENCODE_ALIAS is the routing name
# clients send as "model" to reach the agent; the individual free models are
# addressable by their own ids too (GET /v1/models lists everything).
OPENCODE_ENDPOINT_NAME=opencode
OPENCODE_ALIAS=agent

# ---- provider credentials (what OpenCode uses to reach the models) -------
# launch.sh exports these into the `opencode serve` process, so a key set here
# is the key that provider authenticates with — the alternative to running
# `opencode auth login` once, which is usually easier. The free models relay
# serves come from OpenCode Zen, so OPENCODE_API_KEY is the one that matters.
#OPENCODE_API_KEY=
